# "A Common Pride and Fame": The Attack and Repulse of Pickett's Division, July 3, 1863, Part III (NPS study; typescript, OCR text layer with light noise): the clump of trees (the Copse), Ziegler's Grove and the iron fence (Appendix B, 'The Objective Point of the Assault').
# The study quotes the 1889 trial of the 72nd Pennsylvania against the Memorial Association, Hays's staff officer Shields (1919), Cowan (1913) and Bachelder (1894). Each record names whose account it is; the study's own inferences are marked hedged.
# Quotes are typed in the cleaned form (letters and digits match the text layer; OCR errors such as G3MA for GBMA are given as {{saved|fixed}}); PDF pp. 14 and 15 were looked at as page images and the printed page numbers (437, 438) were read from them.
@ NPS-COMMON-PRIDE-3
%images 14, 15

%basis Captain David Shields, on the staff of General Hays, quoted in Fleming, Life and Letters of Alexander Hays (Pittsburgh, 1919), pp. 451-452 (the study's footnote 16)
15/438 | woodlots | The copse of trees on July 3: a thick growth of thin chestnut oaks little above a horse's head, ground rough, rocky and covered with brambles | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes the account of Captain David Shields of Hays's staff, who rode to the copse on July 3 and found a thick growth of thin chestnut oaks that rose but little above his horse's head, on ground very rough and rocky and covered with brambles | =I rode over at once and found General Webb with his men in the little copse of trees, a thick growth of thin chestnut oaks that rose but little above my horse's head. The ground around the copse was very rough and rocky and covered with brambles, and I was much concerned least my horse should fall and disable me. | #s4
%basis -
15/438 | woodlots | The study's inference: the trees of the copse could not have been more than eight to ten feet high | - | 1863-07-03 | hedged | The study reasons that if the trees or saplings of the copse were thin and reached little above Captain Shields's horse's head they could not have been more than eight to ten feet high | If the trees, or saplings, were >>> eight to ten feet high.
15/438 | woodlots | Ziegler's Grove in 1863: mature white and chestnut oaks and perhaps black cherry, potential height 50 to 100 feet, at least five times the young clump | - | 1863-07-03 | hedged | The study says Ziegler's Grove had mature standing white and chestnut oaks and perhaps some black cherry trees, which have a potential growth in height of 50 to 100 feet, at least five times the height of the young clump of trees | =Ziegler's Grove, however, had mature standing white and chesnut oaks, as well as perhaps some black cherry trees, which have a potential growth | @16: =height of 50 to 100 feet, at least five times the height of the young clump of trees
%basis Cowan, letter of December 5, 1913 to Nicholson, p. 207 (the study's footnote 12)
14/437 | woodlots | Captain Cowan called the copse a bunch of scrub oaks that separated his five guns from the Angle | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes Cowan's later letter that the copse was a bunch of scrub oaks which separated his five guns from the angle where Cushing's Battery was firing | =Cowan described the copse as a bunch of scrub oaks, which separated my five guns from the angle where Cushing's Battery was firing.
%basis Survivors of the 72nd Pa. vs. GBMA, et al. (the trial of 1889), pp. 62, 65 (the study's footnote 13)
14/437 | general | A little hollow in the clump of trees, as if dug out, where six or eight wounded men lay | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes the trial testimony of William H. Porter, 72nd Pennsylvania, that he found some six or eight men lying wounded in a little hollow in the clump of trees, which looked as if it had been dug out | =William H. Porter, Company E of the 72nd Pennsylvania, testified that he found some six or eight men laying wounded in a little hollow in that clump of trees, describing the hollow's appearance as if it had been dug out.
%basis General Webb's testimony in the trial of 1889 (Survivors of the 72nd Pa. vs. GBMA, pp. 168-169, 172), as read by the study (footnote 11)
14/437 | woodlots | Bushes in front of the clump of trees, possibly fifty or sixty yards toward the stone wall | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study quotes Webb's testimony that where some bushes were in front of the clump of trees they were possibly fifty or sixty yards towards the stone wall, and reads from it that the trees running down toward the fence must have been small | =This was because he also described these trees running down to the fence as bushes, viz., it was where some bushes were in front of the clump of trees, they were possibly fifty or sixty yards towards the stone wall. | @13: And Webb himself confirmed this >>> the copse of trees ran down.
%basis E. Stanley Hart, 69th Pennsylvania, testimony in the trial of 1889 (Survivors of the 72nd Pa. vs. GBMA, pp. 80, 81)
