6/429 | woodlots | Ziegler's Grove was a much larger and more striking feature of the 1863 landscape than the clump | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | The study says Ziegler's Grove, a large wooded area north of the clump on the ridgeline itself, was a battlefield landmark and a much larger and striking feature on the 1863 landscape than the clump of trees | Ziegler's Grove, a large wooded area north of the clump >>> on the 1863 landscape.
6/429 | woodlots | Probably no Ziegler's Grove when Bachelder admired the copse after the war; 125 trees replanted there in November 1888 | - | ~1865-1888 (the grove cut after the battle, replanted in November 1888; not a state of 1863) | hedged | The study says there was probably no Ziegler's Grove at the time Bachelder was impressed with the copse of trees and that in November 1888 the Memorial Association replanted 125 trees in the grove, apparently to replace trees cut down there | =He could not, because there was probably no Ziegler's Grove at the time Bachelder was so impressed with the Copse of Trees. In November 1888, just one year after Bachelder managed to get his resolution for the iron fence passed, the {{G3MA|GBMA}} replanted 125 trees in Ziegler's Grove, apparently to replace trees cut down there.
%basis John B. Bachelder, report of February 1, 1894 to Colonel C. H. Buehler, GBMA (the study's footnote 3)
4/427 | woodlots | After the war the owner was cutting the copse trees for rails; Bachelder had him spare them | - | ~1860s (after the war; Bachelder's recollection of 1894) | stated | The study quotes Bachelder's recollection that after the war he found the owner cutting the copse trees down, a dozen or more lying on the ground, and persuaded him to spare them by arguing they were worth only their value as rails | =Later in the season while passing them one day I was shocked to find the owner engaged in cutting them down, a dozen or more already lying on the ground. I expostulated with him, but without effect, until I suggested to him that if he cut them then he was only getting for them their value as rails, whereas, if he allowed them to stand to mark the spot he would eventually get ten times as much for them, and he spared them.
